# Easy Customs API — systemd unit (EC2 / any Linux box).
#
# Install:
#   sudo cp backend/deploy/easycustoms-api.service /etc/systemd/system/
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload
#   sudo systemctl enable --now easycustoms-api
# Watch:
#   journalctl -u easycustoms-api -f
#
# SYSTEMD DOES NOT READ backend/.env, and nothing below tells it to. The file is
# loaded by pydantic-settings from an ABSOLUTE path baked into app/config.py
# (BACKEND_ROOT/".env"), so it is read by the process, not by the unit — which is
# why there is no EnvironmentFile= line and why WorkingDirectory does not decide
# it. Consequence: the file must be readable by User=easycustoms, and a variable
# that is not in it (and not in this unit) is simply absent.
#
# Prerequisites on the box:
#   * the repo at /opt/easycustoms (git clone — never a folder copy),
#     backend/.venv created and `pip install -r requirements.txt` done;
#   * backend/.env. WHAT IT MUST CONTAIN DEPENDS ON THE AUTH PROVIDER, and the
#     two are not variations of one list — see docs/deploy-staging.md for the
#     complete set derived from app/config.py. In brief:
#
#       EASYCUSTOMS_AUTH_PROVIDER=local (the default) — the one operator account
#         IS _AUTH_USERNAME / _AUTH_PASSWORD. Unset, the app fails CLOSED: no
#         token can be issued and every protected route answers 401.
#
#       EASYCUSTOMS_AUTH_PROVIDER=supabase — _AUTH_USERNAME and _AUTH_PASSWORD
#         ARE NOT THE CREDENTIAL SOURCE AND ARE NEVER READ AT SIGN-IN.
#         app/auth.py::verify_login hands the credentials to Supabase's password
#         grant and returns that identity; the local pair is not consulted on any
#         path. What IS required instead is SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_ANON_KEY —
#         the anon/publishable key, never the service-role key — and the app
#         REFUSES TO BOOT without them (config.py::_check_auth_provider_config),
#         so this one is a startup failure rather than a 401.
#         Known cosmetic wart on this path: startup still logs
#         "no login account is configured" from the local-provider check even
#         when supabase is the provider and the message does not apply.
#
#   * EASYCUSTOMS_AUTH_SECRET — required under BOTH providers, and for a reason
#     that only became sharp when --workers went above 1. It signs THIS app's own
#     session token under either provider (Supabase decides identity; the session
#     is this server's). Unset, app/auth.py::_secret falls back to a per-PROCESS
#     random key and logs a warning — so with --workers 2 the two workers sign
#     with DIFFERENT keys, and a session minted by one is rejected as forged by
#     the other. The symptom is not "logged out on restart", it is a browser that
#     is signed in for roughly every other request.
#   * EASYCUSTOMS_SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=always in that .env. Behind a TLS
#     terminator the app sees plain http, so the `auto` default would ship the
#     session cookie WITHOUT `Secure` to a browser that is very much on https.
#     app/main.py::_cookie_secure documents this and names `always` as the
#     answer for exactly this deployment — it is not optional here.
#   * EASYCUSTOMS_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1 in that .env (2 if a CDN sits in front of
#     Caddy). Not optional, and NOW LOAD-BEARING THREE TIMES rather than once:
#     the login throttle, the signup limiter and the password-reset limiter all
#     key on app/auth.py::client_key. At the default of 0 every request keys on
#     Caddy's own address, so all callers share one bucket — 10 failed sign-ins,
#     or the third registration of the day, lock out everybody.
#   * Caddy (or another TLS terminator) in front — see Caddyfile.example.
#     This unit binds LOOPBACK ONLY and is not reachable from the network by
#     itself, which is deliberate: the app speaks plain HTTP.
#   * AWS, when this deployment uses either. Both are the INSTANCE ROLE's job,
#     never keys in .env on a server:
#       EASYCUSTOMS_STORAGE_BACKEND=s3  -> s3:GetObject, s3:PutObject and
#         s3:DeleteObject on <bucket>/<prefix>*. All three: this process stores
#         the upload, serves it back to the evidence panel, and deletes it with
#         the job (app/storage.py). No s3:ListBucket — nothing lists.
#       EASYCUSTOMS_QUEUE_PROVIDER=sqs  -> sqs:SendMessage on the main queue,
#         and nothing else. This process only claims and enqueues; receiving,
#         deleting and extending are the worker's (easycustoms-worker.service).
#   * The metering price file, if costs are wanted. Token and page counts are
#     recorded either way; the money column stays NULL until
#     backend/vendor_prices.json exists (EASYCUSTOMS_USAGE_PRICE_PATH). Under
#     the supabase provider an EXPLICIT
#     EASYCUSTOMS_USAGE_MONTHLY_DOCUMENT_CAP=0 is refused at boot — leave it
#     unset to take the 200/account/month default.
#
# Deploying a schema change (Postgres): migrations are a DELIBERATE step, not
# something this unit does to itself on restart.
#
#   sudo systemctl stop easycustoms-api easycustoms-worker
#   cd /opt/easycustoms/backend && .venv/bin/alembic upgrade head
#   sudo systemctl start easycustoms-api easycustoms-worker
#
# No ExecStartPre runs it, on purpose: an API restart during an incident must
# not silently reshape the database, and two units restarting together would
# race each other applying the same DDL. Both processes refuse to start until
# the schema is at head and say exactly which command to run, so a forgotten
# migration is a loud startup failure rather than a column-missing error on a
# reviewer's screen. A database that predates alembic needs `alembic stamp`
# first — the refusal message spells that case out separately.
#
# Pairs with easycustoms-worker.service. With EASYCUSTOMS_QUEUE_PROVIDER=sqs
# this process stops doing OCR/LLM work entirely and only claims + enqueues;
# run both units. With the queue off, extraction happens inside the request
# here and the worker unit should not be enabled at all.

[Unit]
Description=Easy Customs API (FastAPI/uvicorn)
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=easycustoms
Group=easycustoms
WorkingDirectory=/opt/easycustoms/backend
# --host 127.0.0.1: TLS and the public port belong to Caddy. Binding 0.0.0.0
# here would publish an unencrypted declaration workspace on the VPC.
#
# STILL no --proxy-headers, and now for a better reason than before. uvicorn
# would rewrite request.client.host from X-Forwarded-For, trusting the header
# based on the PEER address (FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS) rather than on how many hops
# it should count — so a caller reaching us through Caddy could still supply a
# leading entry and pick its own throttle key.
#
# app/auth.py::client_key does the same job correctly: it counts
# EASYCUSTOMS_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS entries FROM THE RIGHT, which is the only part
# of the header our own proxies wrote. Set that variable (1 for the Caddy config
# below) and leave this flag off. request.client.host then stays the honest
# socket peer for everything else that reads it.
#
# --workers may now exceed 1. Every piece of process-local state that made more
# workers WRONG rather than merely different was removed on 2026-08-11:
#
#   * the per-job lock is a Postgres transaction-scoped advisory lock, not a
#     threading.Lock, so review/finalize/mutations serialise across processes;
#   * the vendor layout/field-profile stores are database tables, not
#     read-modify-write JSON files that concurrent processes lose entries from;
#   * the login throttle is a database table keyed by a client address read from
#     a trusted position, not a per-process dict — so N workers no longer allow
#     N times the guess rate.
#
# Three things to size together when raising it. EASYCUSTOMS_DB_POOL_SIZE and
# _DB_MAX_OVERFLOW are PER PROCESS, so the connections this unit can open is
# workers x (pool + overflow) — check that against the server's max_connections.
# EASYCUSTOMS_THREADPOOL_MAX_THREADS must not exceed pool + overflow, and that
# one is enforced: config.py refuses to boot on the contradiction rather than
# letting the surplus threads queue on the pool and time out. And in queue mode
# the slow work is not in this process at all, so one worker still goes a long
# way; scale the WORKER unit first.
#
# EASYCUSTOMS_AUTH_SECRET must be set before raising this above 1 — see the
# prerequisite above. Two workers with no configured secret sign sessions with
# two different per-process keys.
#
# EASYCUSTOMS_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1 is required with the Caddy config below (2
# if something like CloudFront sits in front of Caddy). Left at the default 0
# behind a proxy, every request looks like it came from the proxy and all
# callers share ONE throttle bucket: 10 failed logins in 5 minutes locks out
# everybody, which an unauthenticated attacker can trigger deliberately.
ExecStart=/opt/easycustoms/backend/.venv/bin/uvicorn app.main:app \
          --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 --workers 2
Restart=always
RestartSec=5

# QUEUE OFF: a request can hold OCR + LLM for minutes, and killing it mid-run
# leaves a claimed document that only the startup sweep releases. QUEUE ON:
# requests are short and this is generous slack. Sized for the worse case.
TimeoutStopSec=300
KillSignal=SIGTERM

# The app writes storage/ (uploads) and /tmp (Starlette spools multipart parts
# there before any route runs). NOT the vendor layout / field-profile stores:
# those were JSON files under storage/ until 2026-08-11 and are database tables
# now (`vendor_layout`, `vendor_field_profile`) — a whole-file read-modify-write
# loses one writer's entries as soon as a second process records, which
# --workers 2 guarantees. What remains of them here is a one-way READ at startup
# (database.import_legacy_side_stores) of any leftover JSON.
#
# storage/ is still created and still needs to be writable with
# EASYCUSTOMS_STORAGE_BACKEND=s3 — get_settings() mkdirs it unconditionally —
# it just stops receiving uploads.
#
# ProtectSystem=strict MAKES EVERYTHING ELSE READ-ONLY, INCLUDING THE REST OF
# THE REPO. That is deliberate and it has one consequence worth stating rather
# than discovering: the DEFAULT SQLite database path is
# backend/easy_customs.db, which is NOT under storage/, so a SQLite deployment
# under this unit cannot create or write its database (nor the -wal/-shm files,
# which need the directory writable, not just the file). The fix belongs in
# .env, not here — point the URL inside the one writable path:
#   EASYCUSTOMS_DATABASE_URL=sqlite:////opt/easycustoms/backend/storage/easy_customs.db
# Widening ReadWritePaths to the whole backend tree instead would make the venv
# and the application code writable by the service, which is the opposite trade.
# Postgres deployments are unaffected.
NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=true
ReadWritePaths=/opt/easycustoms/backend/storage

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
